{
"slug": "how-to-prepare-for-a-house-clearance",
"cluster": "Planning & costs",
"title": "How to Prepare for a House Clearance | HouseClear",
"meta": "How to get ready for a house clearance: what to set aside, what to tell the team, access and parking, and what happens on the day. A practical checklist.",
"h1": "How to prepare for a house clearance",
"lead": "A little preparation makes a clearance quicker, cheaper and far less stressful. Here is what to do before the team arrives.",
"date": "2026-11-16",
"body": [
("", [
 "<strong>To prepare for a house clearance, decide what is staying, move or label those items, find any important documents, and tell the clearance team about access, parking and anything heavy or unusual.</strong> You do not need to box things up, empty cupboards or take furniture apart. That is the team's job. Your job is to make sure nothing leaves the house that should have stayed.",
]),
("Decide what is staying", [
 "This is the step that matters most. Walk through every room, including the loft, garage and shed, and decide what you or the family want to keep. Then make it impossible to get wrong: move those items into one room and close the door, or label them clearly. A written list helps too, especially if several relatives are involved.",
 "If you are not sure about something, keep it for now. An item can always be cleared later, but it cannot be brought back. If only some rooms need emptying, a <a href='/services/partial-house-clearance/'>partial house clearance</a> is priced on what actually goes.",
]),
("Find the paperwork and valuables first", [
 "Before clearance day, look for the things that are easy to lose among ordinary contents: wills, deeds, bank and pension paperwork, passports, photographs, jewellery, watches, cash and keys. Check the usual hiding places such as bedside drawers, coat pockets, biscuit tins, the backs of wardrobes and under mattresses.",
 "A careful clearance team sets aside documents and valuables it comes across, and we do this on every job. It is still much better to have looked yourself first, because you know what you are looking for.",
]),
("Tell the team what they are walking into", [
 "A fixed quote depends on the team knowing the job. Mention anything that affects time or disposal: a full loft or garage, a piano or safe, fridges and freezers, a property that has been empty for a long time, stairs without a lift, or a long carry from where the van can park. Photos or a short video of every room are the easiest way to do this. Our guide to <a href='/quote/video/'>getting a quote from a phone video</a> explains what to film.",
 "Parking matters more than people expect. If the street needs a permit, or the nearest space is a long way from the door, say so when you book so it can be planned.",
]),
("Sort out the practical details", [
 "Agree how the team will get in. If you cannot be there, keys can be left with a neighbour, solicitor or estate agent. Our guide on <a href='/guides/do-i-need-to-be-there-for-a-house-clearance/'>whether you need to attend a house clearance</a> covers how that works. Let neighbours know if a van will be outside for the day.",
 "Leave the electricity on if you can, so the team has light and can check loft spaces safely. If the property is being sold or handed back to a landlord, tell the team the deadline when you book rather than on the day.",
]),
("What you do not need to do", [
 "You do not need to pack, bag, dismantle or carry anything. You do not need to sort recycling from rubbish, because every load is sorted before disposal and reusable items are donated or resold. And you do not need to clean first. A <a href='/services/full-house-clearance/'>full house clearance</a> leaves the property swept and ready for the next step.",
 "If you want to know what the job is likely to cost before you start, see <a href='/guides/how-much-does-a-house-clearance-cost/'>how much a house clearance costs</a>.",
]),
],
"faqs": [
 ("Do I need to empty cupboards and drawers before a house clearance?", "No. The team empties everything as part of the job. The only thing worth doing yourself is removing or labelling what you want to keep, and checking for documents and valuables."),
 ("How do I make sure nothing important gets cleared by mistake?", "Move everything you are keeping into one room and close the door, or label it clearly, and give the team a written list. Point the room out at the start of the day if you are there, or tell us when you book if you are not."),
 ("Should I sell furniture before the clearance?", "You can, but you do not have to. Where contents have resale value, we offset it against the price of the clearance, which is usually less effort than selling items one at a time."),
 ("How far ahead should I book?", "Most clearances can be booked within a few days. If you have a completion date or a tenancy deadline, tell us as early as you can so the date is held."),
],
"related_services": ["house-clearance", "full-house-clearance", "partial-house-clearance"],
},
{
"slug": "do-i-need-to-be-there-for-a-house-clearance",
"cluster": "Planning & costs",
"title": "Do I Need to Be There for a House Clearance? | HouseClear",
"meta": "You don't have to attend a house clearance. How key collection, photo updates and remote clearances work for executors, landlords and distant families.",
"h1": "Do I need to be there during a house clearance?",
"lead": "No. Many clearances are arranged entirely by phone by people who live hours away. Here is how it works.",
"date": "2026-11-16",
"body": [
("", [
 "<strong>No, you do not need to be there during a house clearance.</strong> As long as the team can get in and knows exactly what is staying and what is going, the whole job can be arranged remotely: quote, keys, clearance and paperwork. This is common for executors who live in another part of the country, for landlords, and for families who would simply rather not be present.",
]),
("How a remote clearance works", [
 "First, the quote. Someone local, such as a relative, neighbour or agent, takes photos or a short video of each room and sends them over, and you get a fixed price back. Our <a href='/quote/video/'>video quote guide</a> explains what to film. If nobody can do that, we can arrange a free visit using a key held locally.",
 "Second, the keys. We can collect them from a solicitor, estate agent, letting agent or neighbour, and return them the same way when the job is finished.",
 "Third, the instructions. You tell us in writing what is being kept and where it is, and anything specific you want us to look out for. Then we clear the property, send photos when it is done, and email the waste transfer note and invoice.",
]),
("What to agree before the day", [
 "Because you will not be there to answer questions, the instructions need to be clear. Agree which items are staying and where they will be, whether anything should be set aside for collection by family, who holds the keys before and after, and who the team should phone if something unexpected turns up.",
 "It also helps to say what should happen with personal items found during the clearance. Documents, photographs and valuables are set aside as standard, and we can post them, leave them at the property or hand them to your solicitor.",
]),
("When it is worth being there", [
 "There are times when attending, at least for the first half hour, is the better choice. If the family has not yet been through the house, if several relatives disagree about what is being kept, or if you simply are not sure what is in the loft or garage, a walk round with the team at the start avoids mistakes.",
 "For a <a href='/services/bereavement-house-clearance/'>bereavement clearance</a>, some people want to be present and some would find it too hard. Either is fine, and you can also leave once the team has started.",
]),
("Executors and solicitors", [
 "Remote clearances are routine in probate. We can work directly with the solicitor handling the estate, collect keys from the firm, address the invoice to the estate and supply the waste transfer note for the file. See <a href='/services/probate-house-clearance/'>probate house clearance</a> and our <a href='/guides/house-clearance-checklist-for-executors/'>checklist for executors</a>.",
]),
],
"faqs": [
 ("How do I know the house was actually cleared if I am not there?", "You receive photos of the property when the job is finished, along with a digital waste transfer note showing where the waste went and a VAT invoice. If an agent or solicitor holds the keys, they can check too."),
 ("Who can hold the keys?", "A solicitor, estate agent, letting agent, neighbour or relative. We collect from them and return the keys to them, or to whoever you nominate, once the property is clear and locked."),
 ("What if the team finds something valuable or personal?", "It is set aside, not cleared. We tell you what was found and you decide whether it is posted, left at the property or handed to your solicitor."),
 ("Can the quote be done without me visiting the property?", "Yes. Photos or a phone video taken by anyone with access are enough for a fixed quote in most cases. If that is not possible, we can visit using a key held locally."),
],
"related_services": ["probate-house-clearance", "end-of-tenancy-clearance", "full-house-clearance"],
},
{
"slug": "clearing-a-house-after-a-death",
"cluster": "Probate & bereavement",
"title": "Clearing a House After a Death: First Steps | HouseClear",
"meta": "What to do first when clearing a home after a death: securing the property, finding documents, insurance, and when the clearance itself can start.",
"h1": "Clearing a house after a death: what to do first",
"lead": "There is no need to rush the clearance itself. A few practical steps in the first weeks protect the property and make everything afterwards easier.",
"date": "2026-11-16",
"body": [
("", [
 "<strong>In the first weeks after a death, the priority is to secure the property, find the important documents and tell the insurer the house is empty. The clearance itself can usually wait until the family is ready and the person dealing with the estate has the authority to act.</strong> This guide covers the practical steps in order. It is general guidance, not legal advice, and a solicitor can advise on your own situation.",
]),
("Secure the property", [
 "Make sure doors and windows are locked, collect any spare keys from neighbours or carers, and remove obvious valuables such as jewellery, cash and bank cards to somewhere safe. Keep a note of what was taken and by whom, so the estate can be accounted for properly later.",
 "Empty the fridge and bins, and in winter leave the heating on low to protect the pipes. Ask a neighbour to keep an eye on the house and clear post from the doormat, since a pile of post tells people the house is empty.",
]),
("Tell the home insurer", [
 "Contact the home insurer early and tell them the policyholder has died and the property is unoccupied. Many home insurance policies limit or remove cover once a house has been empty for a set period, and some set conditions such as regular inspections. Check what the policy says and keep a record of the conversation.",
]),
("Find the documents", [
 "Look for the will, and for paperwork that shows what the person owned and owed: bank and building society statements, pension and insurance letters, share certificates, property deeds, utility bills and tax letters. Check drawers, files, handbags, coat pockets and anywhere the person kept things safe.",
 "Do not throw paperwork away at this stage, even if it looks old. The person dealing with the estate may need it. Our <a href='/guides/house-clearance-checklist-for-executors/'>house clearance checklist for executors</a> lists what to look for room by room.",
]),
("Do not clear the house too soon", [
 "It is natural to want to get on with things, but the contents of the house are part of the estate. As a general rule they should not be sold, given away or cleared until the executor or administrator has the authority to deal with them, and until any valuation needed for probate has been done. Family members taking keepsakes early is a common cause of disputes.",
 "You can still plan ahead. A clearance company can visit, quote and hold a date so the work happens promptly once you are ready. Our guide to <a href='/guides/clearing-a-house-during-probate/'>clearing a house during probate</a> explains the timing in more detail.",
]),
("Go through the house as a family first", [
 "Before any clearance, give the family time to go through the house and choose what they want to keep. Agree it together where you can, and write it down. Photographs, letters and small personal items matter far more than furniture, so look for those first.",
 "If going through everything feels like too much, it does not have to happen in one go. A <a href='/services/bereavement-house-clearance/'>bereavement house clearance</a> can be done in stages, a room at a time, at whatever pace suits you.",
]),
("When you are ready for the clearance", [
 "Once the family has taken what it wants and the executor is able to act, the clearance itself is usually the quickest part. Items the family is keeping are set to one side, reusable furniture and household goods are donated or resold, and the rest is recycled or disposed of with a waste transfer note for the estate's records. <a href='/guides/what-happens-to-belongings-after-a-house-clearance/'>What happens to belongings after a house clearance</a> explains where everything goes.",
 "If the estate is going through probate, see our <a href='/services/probate-house-clearance/'>probate house clearance</a> service, which includes invoices addressed to the estate.",
]),
],
"faqs": [
 ("How soon after a death should a house be cleared?", "There is no fixed time. Secure the property and deal with insurance and documents straight away, but the clearance itself can wait until the family is ready and the executor or administrator has authority to deal with the contents. A rented home or care home room may need to be returned sooner, so check with the landlord or home."),
 ("Can family take items from the house before probate?", "The contents belong to the estate, so as a general rule nothing should be removed until the executor has authority and any valuation has been done. Taking valuables into safekeeping is sensible, as long as a record is kept. A solicitor can advise on your situation."),
 ("What should I do with the person's paperwork?", "Keep all of it until the estate has been dealt with. Bank, pension, tax and property papers may be needed by the executor or solicitor. Anything no longer needed afterwards should be shredded, not put in general waste."),
 ("Can you clear a house in stages?", "Yes. Many families ask us to clear a room at a time, or to remove furniture first and come back for the rest. We work at whatever pace suits you."),
],
"related_services": ["bereavement-house-clearance", "probate-house-clearance", "full-house-clearance"],
},
{
"slug": "how-to-help-clear-a-hoarders-home",
"cluster": "Specialist clearances",
"title": "How to Help Clear a Hoarder's Home | HouseClear",
"meta": "Practical, respectful steps for clearing a hoarded home: safety checks, working with the person, what to keep, and when to bring in a clearance team.",
"h1": "How to help clear a hoarder's home",
"lead": "Clearing a hoarded home is as much about the person as the property. Going carefully, and with their agreement, is what makes it last.",
"date": "2026-11-16",
"body": [
("", [
 "<strong>To help clear a hoarder's home, start with the person's agreement, deal with safety hazards first, make decisions in small steps, and bring in a professional clearance team once the person is ready for items to leave.</strong> Clearing everything in one day without their involvement rarely works for long, and can cause real distress. This guide is practical advice from doing these clearances, not medical advice.",
]),
("Understand what you are dealing with", [
 "Hoarding disorder is a recognised mental health condition. The items that look like rubbish to you may feel important, or even essential, to the person who kept them, and being told to throw them away can feel like an attack. Shame is common, and so is fear of being judged by whoever comes through the door.",
 "That is why the first step is a conversation, not a skip. If the person has not yet spoken to anyone about it, their GP is the usual starting point, and there are UK charities that support people who hoard and their families. The local council's adult social care or environmental health team can also advise where there is a risk to safety.",
]),
("Put safety first", [
 "Before any sorting starts, look at the risks. In a hoarded home the common ones are fire, because exits are blocked and items are stacked near heaters or cookers; trips and falling stacks; rotten food and pests; damp and mould; and sharp or broken items hidden in piles. In serious cases the sheer weight of contents can affect floors.",
 "The first goal is a safe home, not an empty one: clear routes to the doors, access to the kitchen, bathroom and bed, working smoke alarms, and nothing piled against heat sources. Wear gloves and sturdy shoes, and do not go into lofts or rooms where the floor is in doubt.",
]),
("Agree the plan with the person", [
 "Where the person still lives in the home, involve them in every decision you can. Agree which room or area comes first, and keep sessions short. Starting with something that is clearly rubbish, such as spoiled food or old packaging, builds trust before harder decisions.",
 "Simple categories help: keep, donate, recycle, dispose. Avoid a 'maybe' pile, which tends to become the new hoard. Agree in advance what is not to be touched, and stick to it. Breaking that agreement once can undo weeks of progress.",
]),
("When the home is empty or the person has died", [
 "Many hoarder clearances are arranged by families after someone has moved into care or died. The emotional side is different, but the practical challenges are the same, with one addition: valuables and documents are very often buried among ordinary items. Cash in envelopes, jewellery in food tins and important paperwork between newspapers are all things we come across.",
 "For that reason a hoarded property should be searched as it is cleared, not simply emptied. If the estate is going through probate, see <a href='/guides/clearing-a-house-during-probate/'>clearing a house during probate</a> before anything is removed.",
]),
("When to bring in a clearance team", [
 "Families often start themselves and then find the scale is beyond what a few car loads to the tip can handle. Hoarded homes usually hold far more than they appear to, and some of it needs careful handling. A professional <a href='/services/hoarder-house-clearance/'>hoarder house clearance</a> brings a team used to working discreetly, searching as it goes, and disposing of everything legally with a waste transfer note.",
 "A quote depends on volume and condition, so photos or a short video of each room are the best starting point. Our guide to <a href='/guides/how-much-does-a-house-clearance-cost/'>how much a house clearance costs</a> explains why hoarded properties are priced individually. It can also be done in stages, a room or a floor at a time, if that suits the person better.",
]),
("Afterwards", [
 "A cleared home is not the end of it. Without support, the hoarding often returns. Regular contact, help from the GP or a support group, and small routines for dealing with post and shopping all make it more likely the home stays safe.",
]),
],
"faqs": [
 ("Can a hoarder's house be cleared without their permission?", "If the person lives there and has the capacity to decide, their belongings are theirs and a clearance needs their agreement. Where there is a serious risk to health or safety, the council's environmental health or adult social care team can advise on what can be done. A clearance company should not clear an occupied home against the occupant's wishes."),
 ("How long does it take to clear a hoarded house?", "Longer than a normal clearance of the same size, because there is far more volume and the contents need searching as they are removed. It depends on the property, so we quote each one individually after seeing photos, a video or the property itself."),
 ("Will the neighbours know what is happening?", "We work discreetly and do not discuss the job with anyone but the client. If discretion is a particular worry, tell us when you enquire and we will plan around it."),
 ("What happens to things of value found during the clearance?", "They are set aside and handed to you or to the person you nominate. Searching for documents, cash and valuables is a standard part of a hoarder clearance, because they are so often hidden among ordinary items."),
],
"related_services": ["hoarder-house-clearance", "full-house-clearance", "bereavement-house-clearance"],
},
